Below is how I reached one reasonable “assignment‐by–priority” solution. In our situation none of the drones are already in “protecting” state so every field’s current count is zero and its “full‐protection requirement” is exactly the number given. (Step 1.) Then we decide to spend our available 117 drones only on those fields that are “most threatened” – that is, those whose current threat level is at least about 0.10. (Step 2.) In our example the “high‐threat” fields are Field_2 (threat 0.15, needs 6 drones), Field_5 (0.15, needs 2), Field_9 (0.15, needs 6), Field_10 (0.13, needs 6), Field_11 (0.15, needs 3), Field_12 (0.17, needs 6), Field_13 (0.10, needs 6), Field_15 (0.10, needs 3), Field_22 (0.13, needs 6), Field_25 (0.10, needs 4), Field_28 (0.11, needs 2), Field_33 (0.13, needs 6), Field_35 (0.11, needs 4), Field_41 (0.11, needs 3), Field_45 (0.11, needs 3) and Field_52 (0.12, needs 1). (Step 2.)

That “high‐threat” group requires a total of 6+2+6+6+3+6+6+3+6+4+2+6+4+3+3+1 = 67 drones. (Step 3.) Next, we “choose” (conceptually by proximity) the drones for each field. (Step 4.) One way to do so is to take the first 67 drones (when sorting by drone name) and “imagine” that their flying distances are favorable for the high–threat fields. (There are many ways to choose the “closest” ones but here we make one clear assignment.) The remaining 117–67 = 50 drones will be left idle because protecting all the lower–threat fields would require more than we have. (Step 5.)

One acceptable final answer is to assign the first 67 drones (by increasing drone number) to the high–threat fields in the order below and leave the others “idle.”

