Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and then the final assignment. 

Step 1. For each field we first list the “in‐place” protecting drones and compare with the “full protection” requirement. For example, Field_1 needs 2 protectors but already has 9 drones flying to it. We decide to “choose” the two closest among them (here Drone_113 and Drone_104) and “cancel” the surplus so that those drones are reassigned (set to idle). A similar analysis was done for every field. In a number of cases (Fields with very low threat or already fully protected such as Field_2, Field_5, Field_8, Field_9) we simply keep the drones already protecting the field and later set drones en route but not “needed” to idle. 

Step 2. Then we decided which fields really require protection. In our example we protect only fields whose bird threat is at least about 0.10 (or already in a protection‐complete status) so that the most “at risk” fields – for example Field_10 (0.31), Field_13 (0.35), Field_14 (0.30) – are chosen for new assignments. 

Step 3. Next, for each field chosen we “lock in” the drones that are already protecting it. For example, Field_9 already has six drones protecting it and so we leave Drone_10, Drone_11, Drone_13, Drone_36, Drone_37 and Drone_108 “in‐place.” 

Step 4. For each field needing additional drones we “choose” (by estimating center–to–drone distance) the set of drones – from those idle or coming toward fields whose target is not needed – that are closest to the field’s center. For example, for Field_10 (whose center is about (91.5,84)) we picked the six closest among the available idle ones (Drone_46, Drone_55, Drone_28, Drone_3, Drone_20 and Drone_100). In a similar way we have “trimmed” the extra drones that were en route for fields (like Field_1, Field_3, Field_12, Field_13 and Field_14) so that only as many as needed remain assigned while the others are re–assigned as idle.

Step 5. Finally, we give every drone exactly one group assignment. Drones chosen for protection keep the group “protecting Field_X” where X is the field they protect. Every drone that was not “chosen” to help a prioritized field (or that were in transit to a non–needed field) is set to “idle.”

Below is the final group assignment for each Drone. (Any drone that was originally “moving_to_field” for a field that we did not “use” in our protection plan has been reassigned to idle.)

