Below is one “walk‐through” of our thinking and then the final listing. (Note that many drones were “re‐assigned” away from their original target when we decided that only the “most threatened” fields would get extra help. For example, although Field_5 already had two drones protecting it (and so did Field_2 and Field_9) many drones originally “moving_to_field” those low‐threat fields were “freed” and then re–directed to higher‐threat fields such as Field_10, Field_11, Field_13, Field_14, Field_17, Field_19, Field_20, Field_21 and Field_22. Also, even though full “coverage” might require a certain number of drones (for instance Field_33 requires six but we could only assign four because there simply were not enough “closer” drones available now after using them elsewhere), we decided to put available “closest” drones on the fields in order of priority.) 

Step 1. For every field we checked which drones are already “protecting” that field according to the drone list. For example, Field_1 already had one protecting (Drone_113) while Field_2 already had six protecting (Drones_25, 29, 31, 60, 61 and 79). Then we computed how many extra drones would be needed (for Field_1 full protection requires 2 so one extra is needed; for Field_3 it requires 3 so with two already protecting [Drone_27 and Drone_30] we needed one more; etc.).

Step 2. Because far too many fields have nonzero threat levels but there are not enough drones to cover all, we decided that only fields with a “high” threat (roughly those with threat level >0.10 or that are borderline and nearly lacking any protection) would receive extra assignments. That is why although Field_4 (threat=0.03) already has nine drones “flying toward” it, we chose not to add extra protection there. In contrast, fields such as Field_10 (threat=0.22), Field_11 (threat=0.12), Field_13 (threat=0.29), Field_14 (threat=0.29), Field_17 (0.15), Field_19 (0.20), Field_20 (0.21), Field_21 (0.22) and Field_22 (0.35) were given priority.

Step 3. For each field chosen for protection we “locked in” the drones that were already protecting that field (for example, Field_6 already has its three protectors, so no extra were needed).

Step 4. Then, for each field that needed extra protection, we “picked” – from among those drones not already locked into protecting some high‐priority field – the ones whose current (or last known) location was closest to the centre of that field. For example, for Field_1 (whose centre is roughly (42,26)) we looked at the drones whose positions were nearest; we then reassigned one extra drone (in this case Drone_104) to “protect Field_1.” Similarly, because Field_10 (with centre roughly (91.5,84)) needed six protectors, we selected six idle drones that lay closest in location (for example Drone_39, Drone_67, Drone_68, Drone_101, Drone_40 and Drone_107). (In several cases the “closest‐by” drones were originally speeding toward another, unwanted field – and we then re–directed them to protect the more threatened one.) 

Step 5. Finally we produced one line per drone so that any drone not “picked” (or that lost its originally planned target because that field had low threat or was already “full”) is set to “idle.”

Below is our final group assignment. (Remember: each drone is listed with exactly one group – either its new “protecting Field_x” group or “idle.”)

