Step 1 – Determining Protection Requirements for Each Field 
• Field_1 has a full‐protection requirement of 2 drones and already has 2 protecting (Drone_104 and Drone_113), so it needs 0 additional drones. 
• Field_11 (threat 0.14) needs 3 drones (full requirement 3 – 0 already protecting). 
• Field_13 (threat 0.19) needs 6 drones. 
• Field_14 (threat 0.25) needs 9 drones. 
• Field_20 (threat 0.15) needs 3 drones. 
• Field_21 (threat 0.11) needs 6 drones. 
• Field_22 (threat 0.10) needs 6 drones. 
• Field_29 (threat 0.14) needs 6 drones. 
• Field_34 (threat 0.13) needs 4 drones. 
• Field_38 (threat 0.11) needs 3 drones. 
• Field_43 (threat 0.12) needs 2 drones. 
• Field_17 (threat 0.10) needs 6 drones. 
• Field_63 (threat 0.10) needs 1 drone. 

Step 2 – Which Fields Are Chosen for Protection 
Because there are not enough drones to cover every field (and because many fields have very low threat values) the coordinator decides to devote the fleet to the fields with the highest threat levels. In this solution the fields that “need protection” are those with threat ≳0.10. These chosen fields are: Field_1, Field_11, Field_13, Field_14, Field_20, Field_21, Field_22, Field_29, Field_34, Field_38, Field_43, Field_17, and Field_63. (Note that Field_1 is already fully covered.) All drones originally “moving to” or “protecting” fields that are not on this prioritized list are re‐assigned as idle.

Step 3 – Retaining Already Protecting Drones 
• Field_1 retains its two protecting drones (Drone_104 and Drone_113). 
• All other fields had no drones already in “protecting” status (Field_9’s one protecting drone, Drone_66, is now dropped since Field_9 is not chosen). 

Step 4 – Assigning the Additional Drones 
For each field that needs protection the coordinator “picks” as many drones as necessary from the available pool (drones in a “moving_to_field” or “idle” state) based roughly on closeness to the field’s center (the center of a rectangular field is used as the target location). For brevity an approximate assignment was made as follows (using approximate centers – for example Field_14’s center is about (43,99); Field_22’s center about (98,52); etc.). 

The following assignments were chosen:
• Field_1 (center ≈ (42,26)): already fully protected by Drone_104 and Drone_113.
• Field_11 (center ≈ (188,139)): assign Drone_82, Drone_83, and Drone_92.
• Field_13 (center ≈ (161.5,98)): assign Drone_41, Drone_42, Drone_43, Drone_44, Drone_45, and Drone_46.
• Field_14 (center ≈ (43,99)): from drones originally headed for Field_1 choose those whose positions are “closer” to (43,99): assign Drone_1, Drone_2, Drone_3, Drone_35, Drone_36, and Drone_37; then add three additional drones chosen from idle – here we pick Drone_73, Drone_106, and Drone_116 – to reach 9 total.
• Field_20 (center ≈ (83,23)): assign Drone_38 (whose position (88,47) is a very good fit), Drone_18, and Drone_30.
• Field_21 (center ≈ (42.5,54.5)): assign two drones from the remaining original Field_1 group (Drone_39 and Drone_40) plus idle drones with relatively low x–values: assign Drone_77, Drone_78, Drone_84, and Drone_86.
• Field_22 (center ≈ (98,52)): from drones originally heading toward Field_2 (and nearby) assign Drone_4, Drone_5, Drone_7, Drone_8, and Drone_9; then add Drone_16 (whose (106,54) is very near the center) to total 6.
• Field_29 (center ≈ (133,165)): assign from available drones those with relatively higher x and y – for example, from among drones originally heading to Field_7 and those still available, assign Drone_29, Drone_31, and also use idle drones that are “close” in a relative sense: assign Drone_79, Drone_67, Drone_87, and Drone_88.
• Field_34 (center ≈ (48.5,158)): choose idle drones with low x and high y: assign Drone_89, Drone_91, Drone_93, and Drone_108.
• Field_38 (center ≈ (68.5,30.5)): assign from a mix of remaining near‐by drones – for instance, from the Field_4 group pick Drone_17 and Drone_23 and add an idle drone with a low y coordinate (Drone_105) to total 3.
• Field_43 (center ≈ (18,82)): assign idle drones with very low x; here choose Drone_51 and Drone_55.
