Step 1 – Identify current protection and extra‐needed numbers by field:
• Field_1 needs 2 drones and already has 2 protecting (Drone_104 and Drone_113). No extra protection is needed.
• Field_2 needs 6 drones. Although one drone (Drone_9) is “protecting” and five are “moving_to_field” (Drones_4, 5, 7, 8, 16), the very low threat (0.01) makes it a low‐priority candidate for protection.
• Field_3 needs 3 drones. Nine drones are “moving_to_field” for Field_3 but we “choose” the three that are closest to the small Field_3 area (we assume that means picking Drones_34, 55 and 51) and the rest are not needed.
• Field_4 needs 2 drones. With nine drones in flight for Field_4 we choose the two that are closest (here we select Drones_3 and 35) and the others can be freed.
• Field_8 needs 1 drone; among the movers we pick the one that is closest (Drone_17) leaving the others available.
• Field_9 needs 6 drones and exactly six are “moving_to_field” (Drones_29, 31, 67, 79, 87 and 88).
• Field_11 (threat 0.14, high‐priority) needs 3 drones; none are currently protecting it so we will reassign one of the better‐located drones from a low‐threat field (we choose Drones_8, 92 and 77).
• Field_13 (threat 0.16, high–priority) needs 6 drones; we assign the six that are closest (we select Drones_20, 24, 28, 32, 33 and 50).
• Field_14 (threat 0.20, highest priority) needs 9 drones; none are coming so we assign 9 of the “idle” (or freed) drones that are relatively near the field (we choose Drones_12, 13, 14, 15, 27, 41, 42, 43 and 44).
• Field_20 (threat 0.12, high‐priority) needs 3 drones; we assign the 3 closest (here, Drones_62, 16 and 1).
