Step 2 – Decide on protection priorities:
Because there are not enough drones to cover every field we concentrate our available resources on the fields with highest threat levels. In priority order these become the high‐threat fields (Field_14, Field_13, Field_11, Field_38, Field_20, then the group of fields with threat near 0.11 [Field_21, Field_29, Field_39, Field_66]). Fields that already are protected (or almost fully “in‐the‐air”) but have low threat (Fields_1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 9) are left as is (or re–assigned so that extra drones can be used for high–priority fields). In particular we “let” Field_1 through Field_4, Field_8 and Field_9 have their “chosen” protecting drones and reassign any extra drones that had been flying toward a low–threat field (for example in Field_2) to “idle”.

Step 3 – For each high–priority field the drones already “protecting” remain:
• Field_1 already has its 2 protecting drones.
• The other high–priority fields had no drones already protecting them.

Step 4 – Assign additional drones to reach full protection on the high–priority fields (and “free” drones that were moving to a low–priority field):
