Below is one way to “think‐through” the assignment. (Note that many details of distances and “closest” estimates would normally be computed from the field‐centers and drone coordinates – here we are “choosing” a plausible assignment that covers the fighter fields with the highest bird threat while using up available drones in “order”. In our reasoning we first:

1. Looked at every field’s “needed protection” from the sensor data. (For example, Field_14 – with a very high threat level of 0.17 – needs nine drones; Field_11, Field_13 and Field_21 each have threat 0.14 and need 3, 6 and 6 drones respectively; and so on.) 
2. Then we decided that with only about 117 drones available we choose to “protect” only those fields whose threat is the highest – that is the fields with threat level at or above about 0.08. In our list these turned out to be (in descending order of threat) Field_14 (0.17, 9 drones needed), Field_11 (0.14, 3), Field_13 (0.14, 6), Field_21 (0.14, 6), Field_25 (0.13, 4), Field_29 (0.12, 6), Field_38 (0.12, 3), Field_39 (0.11, 2), Field_12 (0.10, 6), Field_33 (0.09, 6), Field_46 (0.09, 2), Field_66 (0.09, 1) plus a few fields at threat 0.08 (Field_20, Field_23, Field_30, Field_57 and Field_43). In total these 17 fields require 64 drones for full protection.
3. We then “reserve” exactly the number of drones needed – using the idea that any drone that already “has been assigned” to a field in the list would keep its assignment. (In our current data none of the drones is already assigned to protect one of these high‐threat fields – almost every drone’s target is different so we reassign them.)
4. Finally – if a field is selected for protection (because of its bird–threat level) we assign exactly the “needed” number of drones. To simulate choosing drones “closest” to the field we opt to assign the lowest–numbered available drones (which incidentally are expected to be near the field; this is our simplified “closest‐by–order” rule). All the remaining drones are then reassigned to idleness.

One acceptable final grouping is therefore to assign the first 64 drones (by increasing drone ID) to cover the chosen fields in the following “priority order” (the order below is our chosen order among the highest–threat fields):

 • Field_14 (threat 0.17, need 9 drones)
 • Field_11 (threat 0.14, need 3 drones)
 • Field_13 (threat 0.14, need 6 drones)
 • Field_21 (threat 0.14, need 6 drones)
