Step 1. For each field we first “tally” the drones that are already “protecting” (or in‐flight with a target) and note how many drones are needed for full protection. For example, Field_4 (threat 0.03) already has nine drones (Drone_1–Drone_9) en route though only 2 are needed, so it is “over‐protected.” In contrast, many fields with high threat (for example, Field_14 with threat 0.23, which requires 9 drones) currently have no drones assigned at all. (In our “tally” we list the fields that already have drones with a “moving_to_field” or “protecting” state – for Field_1 we see nine drones (Drone_10, Drone_11, Drone_12, Drone_19, Drone_20, Drone_21, Drone_22, Drone_23, Drone_24), for Field_3 we see 15 drones (including one already “protecting” Drone_51), for Field_9 we see 8 drones, etc.) 

Step 2. Next we decide which fields really “need protection.” Because there are not enough drones to cover every field, we keep protection for those fields that have a nonzero threat level and (especially) the highest threat values. In our case the fields that already have a set of drones en route (or protecting) – Field_1, Field_2, Field_3, Field_4, Field_6, Field_7, Field_8, Field_9, Field_68 – are left as‐is. In addition, a set of high‐threat fields that are not yet being protected (such as Field_14 [threat 0.23, 9 needed], Field_12 [0.18, 6 needed], Field_38 [0.15, 3 needed], Field_11 [0.14, 3 needed], Field_21 [0.14, 6 needed], Field_13 [0.13, 6 needed] and Field_25 [0.13, 4 needed]) are “chosen” for protection using additional “idle” drones selected on the basis of proximity to the field’s center. Fields with threat 0 – for example Field_5 – are not prioritized; any drones originally heading there are re‐assigned as idle.

Step 3. For each field needing protection we “lock in” those drones already protecting it. (For example, Field_3’s Drone_51 and Field_9’s Drones_29 and 31 are kept.)

Step 4. For each such field we “fill in” the remaining drones needed – choosing from idle drones, selecting those with the shortest distance (using each field’s rectangle “center”) to “fill” the need. For Field_14 (center ≈(43,99)) we select nine idle drones – for instance, after comparing many idle drone locations the “closest” we choose are Drone_100, Drone_106, Drone_102, Drone_68, Drone_115, Drone_73, Drone_103, Drone_77 and Drone_84. In a similar fashion we choose six idle drones for Field_12 (center ≈(115,15)): Drone_104, Drone_113, Drone_96, Drone_92, Drone_117 and Drone_80; three for Field_38 (center ≈(68.5,30.5)): Drone_116, Drone_78 and Drone_90; three for Field_11 (center ≈(188,139.5)): Drone_107, Drone_109 and Drone_112; six for Field_21 (center ≈(42.5,54.5)): Drone_86, Drone_81, Drone_114, Drone_95, Drone_108 and Drone_83; six for Field_13 (center ≈(161.5,98)): Drone_111, Drone_65, Drone_71, Drone_82, Drone_69 and Drone_87; and four for Field_25 (center ≈(192,46.5)): Drone_88, Drone_76, Drone_67 and Drone_94.

Step 5. Finally we give each drone its final group assignment. All drones that were “in flight” with a target for a field that is being protected remain with that field’s final “protect” group. Drones that were originally heading to a field with no need (for example, Field_5 which has threat 0) are re‐assigned to the “idle” group. Any “idle” drones not used to fill a needed field remain idle.

Below is the final list. (All drones not mentioned in an “added‐assignment” step remain with the field they were already moving to.)

