Below is one possible solution after we work through the steps. We first note that – step 1 – none of the fields (except Field_9, which already has 10 drones protecting it even though it only needs 6) currently have any protecting drones. (For example Field_1 needed 2 but had 0 protecting; Field_2 needed 6 but had 0; etc.) Then – step 2 – because we do not have enough drones to “cover” every field, we decide only to “protect” those fields that are under the highest immediate threat. In our reasoning we chose as “priority” the fields whose sensor‐threat levels are highest. For our example we decide to protect the fields with threat levels above roughly 0.10 (plus Field_14 with 0.19, Field_12 and Field_13 with 0.14, etc.) In our solution the “priority” fields (which we assign new protecting groups for) are (with the number of drones they need for full protection):

  • Field_14 (threat = 0.19) needs 9 drones  
  • Field_12 (0.14) needs 6  
  • Field_13 (0.14) needs 6  
  • Field_21 (0.13) needs 6  
  • Field_29 (0.13) needs 6  
  • Field_25 (0.11) needs 4  
  • Field_45 (0.11) needs 3  
  • Field_59 (0.11) needs 2  
  • Field_11 (0.12) needs 3  
  • Field_63 (0.10) needs 1  
  • Field_19 (0.10) needs 3  
  • Field_57 (0.10) needs 1  
  • Field_43 (0.09) needs 2  
  • Field_16 (0.08) needs 1

That is a total of 49 drones to “protect” these high‐threat fields. (Field_9 already is “protected” by 10 drones; we leave those as is.) 

Then – step 3 – for each field that needs protection we “keep” any drones already protecting it. (All fields besides Field_9 have no protecting drones so nothing is pre‐assigned.) 

Then – step 4 – from all of our drones we “choose” the ones closest (we simulate this here by simply taking the idle pool in increasing drone ID order – our heuristic “closest” ordering – to fill the needed slots). For instance, the first 9 idle drones become assigned to Field_14, the next 6 idle drones to Field_12, then the next 6 idle drones to Field_13, and so on. Drones that already were in a “protecting Field_9” group (the ones already in protecting state for Field_9) keep their role. All others that are not needed to secure a priority field are re‐assigned to idle.

In our final assignment we use the following rule: if a drone’s original target is one of our chosen “priority” fields we reassign it to that group if it appears in our “closest” order; if its original target was not one of the protected fields (or if it is extra) then we mark it idle. (Notice that many drones originally “moving_to_field” a nonpriority field – for example Field_1, Field_2, Field_3, Field_5, Field_6, Field_7, Field_8 or Field_68 – are now made idle.) 

