• Field_3 (threat 0.03, needs 3): There are 2 drones already protecting (Drone_20 and Drone_22) so it would need +1.
• Field_4 (threat 0.04, needs 2): No protecting drones (9 are “moving_to_field”).
• Field_5 (threat 0.02, needs 2): No protecting drones (7 are “moving_to_field”).
• Field_6 (threat 0.05, needs 3): No protecting drones (1 is “moving_to_field”).
• Field_7 (threat 0.05, needs 1): No protecting drones (1 is “moving_to_field”).
• Field_9 (threat 0.02, needs 6): There are already 14 protecting drones (Drone_25,27,28,29,31,39,52,66,70,72,74,79,87,91) – far more than needed.
• Fields with much higher threat levels (for example Field_11 with threat 0.12 needing 3; Field_12 and Field_13 with threat 0.16 needing 6 each; Field_14 with threat 0.19 needing 9; Field_20 with threat 0.11 needing 3; Field_21 with threat 0.12 needing 6; Field_22 with threat 0.10 needing 6; Field_25 with threat 0.14 needing 4; Field_29 with threat 0.13 needing 6; Field_43 with threat 0.10 needing 2) have no drones initially assigned. 

Step 2 – Choosing which fields to protect:
Because there are not enough drones to cover every field, we decide to devote protection only where the threat is highest. The fields with threat at or above about 0.10 (and then also re–using over‐assigned Field_9 drones) are chosen for protection. In our plan these are:
 • Field_14 (threat 0.19, needs 9),
 • Field_12 (0.16, needs 6),
 • Field_13 (0.16, needs 6),
 • Field_25 (0.14, needs 4),
 • Field_11 (0.12, needs 3),
 • Field_21 (0.12, needs 6),
 • Field_20 (0.11, needs 3),
 • Field_22 (0.10, needs 6),
 • Field_43 (0.10, needs 2)
 • Field_29 (0.13, needs 6).
All other fields – including those with low threat (Fields 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,9, etc.) – will have no protection assigned (drones will be “idle”). For Field_3, although there are 2 protecting already, the threat is low so we decide not to add the extra needed drone; for Field_9, despite over–protection initially, we reassign extra protecting drones to support the high–threat fields.

Step 3 – Retaining protecting drones on the highest–threat fields:
None of the chosen high–threat fields already had “protecting” drones except Field_9. Because Field_9’s threat is low (0.02) we actually re–assign most of its protecting drones. In our plan we “steal” 2 protecting drones from Field_9 to cover Field_43 and 6 protecting drones to cover Field_29. (Any protecting drones on a field not chosen for high threat – such as the extra protecting in Field_9 and the two protecting on Field_3 – are re–assigned to idle.)

Step 4 – Supplementing drones for full protection by picking the closest available ones:
We choose to “fill” the required protecting counts for the high–threat fields from the idle pool. To do this we list the idle drones (those originally “idle” plus those originally “moving_to_field” that were targeting fields not selected for protection, as well as the extra Field_9 protecting drones we are not “keeping”). In our solution we use the idle drones in increasing Drone number order as a proxy for being “close” (this is our heuristic choice). In other words, we assign the following:

• For Field_14 (needs 9): assign idle drones Drone_64, Drone_65, Drone_67, Drone_68, Drone_69, Drone_71, Drone_73, Drone_75, Drone_76.
• For Field_12 (needs 6): assign idle drones Drone_77, Drone_78, Drone_80, Drone_81, Drone_84, Drone_85.
• For Field_13 (needs 6): assign idle drones Drone_86, Drone_88, Drone_89, Drone_90, Drone_92, Drone_93.
• For Field_25 (needs 4): assign idle drones Drone_94, Drone_95, Drone_96, Drone_97.
• For Field_11 (needs 3): assign idle drones Drone_98, Drone_99, Drone_100.
• For Field_21 (needs 6): assign idle drones Drone_101, Drone_102, Drone_105, Drone_106, Drone_107, Drone_108.
• For Field_20 (needs 3): assign idle drones Drone_109, Drone_110, Drone_111.
• For Field_22 (needs 6): assign idle drones Drone_112, Drone_113, Drone_114, Drone_115, Drone_116, Drone_117.
• For Field_43 (needs 2): re–assign two drones from the over–protected Field_9; choose Drone_25 and Drone_27.
• For Field_29 (needs 6): re–assign six drones from Field_9’s protecting group; choose Drone_28, Drone_29, Drone_31, Drone_39, Drone_52, and Drone_66.

Step 5 – Final group assignment:
All drones originally assigned to a field that we are not protecting (for example drones “moving_to_field” for Fields 1,2,3,4,5,6,7 and the extra Field_9 protecting ones) are set to idle.

Below are the final group assignments, one line per drone, using our chosen allocation. (Any drone not listed in a “protecting Field_X” group is assigned “idle”.)

