Step 1. For every field we first count the drones already “protecting” it (or, for those fields with moving‐to‐field assignments, we know which ones will arrive) and compare with the “for full protection” number. For example, Field_1 needs 2 drones but already has 9 drones en‐route so only 2 of those need to “make it,” and the extra from that group can be re–assigned to other high‐threat fields. (Similarly Field_2 requires 6 but has 12 moving, Field_3 requires 3 but has 8 moving, Field_4 requires 2 but has 9 moving, Field_9 already is “protecting” by 6 drones, and Field_68 requires 2 and currently has 3 moving.) All fields with no drones already “protecting” must be covered by assigning idle drones from our pool once we decide which fields need protection.

Step 2. We then decide which fields need protection. Because the overall threat levels vary we choose to protect first those fields with the highest threat levels. In our situation the fields already being “targeted” (Field_1, Field_2, Field_3, Field_4, Field_9 and Field_68) are automatically protected (using only as many drones as needed). Then among the remaining fields we select the very high‐threat ones – for instance Field_14 (threat 0.21, needing 9), Field_12 (0.19, 6), Field_13 (0.16, 6), Field_27 (0.14, 3), Field_29 (0.13, 6), Field_11 (0.12, 3), Field_25 (0.12, 4) and Field_33 (0.12, 6) plus Field_21 (0.11, 6) and Field_22 (0.10, 6). Then we use the remaining available drones to cover moderately threatened fields. In our count—with 117 total drones (and noting that many are already preassigned in “moving” or “protecting” groups) – after covering the highest‐threat fields and the ones already en–route we assign additional idle drones to other moderate fields by prioritizing those with threat levels in the 0.07–0.08 range. In our plan we cover (from highest down) “protecting” Field_41 (0.08, need 3), Field_56 (0.08, need 2), Field_6 (0.07, need 3), Field_20 (0.07, need 3), Field_24 (0.07, need 2), Field_30 (0.07, need 2), Field_45 (0.07, need 3) and Field_43 (0.07, need 2) plus then for the next 0.06 fields we assign Field_38 (0.06, 3) and Field_10 (0.06, 6). Because the total demand is high we next check the remaining idle drones; we find that for Field_17 (0.06, 6) there are not enough to cover it fully so we skip it. Then we cover Field_19 (0.06, 3) and finally Field_64 (0.06, 2). All other fields (or those with very low threat level, e.g. threat level 0.00 or about 0.02–0.05) are left unassigned (idle).

Step 3. For each field that already has assigned “moving‐to–field” or “protecting” drones, we “lock in” as many as needed for full protection. For Field_1 we choose two out of the nine en–route; for Field_2 we choose six out of 12; for Field_3 we select three out of eight; for Field_4 two out of nine; for Field_9 the six “protecting” drones remain; and for Field_68 we choose two out of three.

Step 4. For each field needing additional drones from the idle pool we “pick” the closest available drones (here we assume that picking by increasing drone number simulates “closest” among a large available pool). We assign, in order of priority, the following idle drones to cover each field fully:
 • Field_14 (threat 0.21, needs 9) gets the idle drones with the smallest numbers.
 • Field_12 (threat 0.19, needs 6) gets the next 6 idle drones.
 • Field_13 (threat 0.16, 6) gets 6.
 • Field_27 (0.14, 3) gets 3.
 • Field_29 (0.13, 6) gets 6.
 • Field_11 (0.12, 3) gets 3.
 • Field_25 (0.12, 4) gets 4.
 • Field_33 (0.12, 6) gets 6.
 • Field_21 (0.11, 6) gets 6.
 • Field_22 (0.10, 6) gets 6.
Then from the remaining idle drones we cover the moderately threatened fields:
 • Field_41 (0.08, 3) gets 3.
 • Field_56 (0.08, 2) gets 2.
 • Field_6 (0.07, 3) gets 3.
 • Field_20 (0.07, 3) gets 3.
 • Field_24 (0.07, 2) gets 2.
 • Field_30 (0.07, 2) gets 2.
 • Field_45 (0.07, 3) gets 3.
