Below is one worked‐through solution. We first “inspect” every field’s current protection and “calculate” how many extra drones would be required if we were to “fully protect” that field. (For example, Field_1 has a full–protection quota of 2 but currently no drone is “protecting” it; however, two drones (Drone_54 and Drone_55) have already “moved” toward Field_1 so we assume that if we want to guarantee full protection we will later want to add two more drones – for the purpose of our high–priority decision we assume Field_1 is “important” because its threat level is 0.09.) 

Then we decide that – because there simply are “not enough drones to protect every field” – we will only use the extra (idle) drones to cover the “high–threat” fields. In our solution we picked as “high–threat” every field whose threat level is roughly at least 0.07. (Thus Field_12, Field_14, Field_22, Field_13, Field_29, Field_25, Field_27, Field_33, Field_68, Field_1, Field_21, Field_66, Field_36, Field_40, Field_56 and Field_59 are “high–threat” and need between 2 and 9 extra drones – see explanation below.) 

Step 1. For each field we identified the drones already “protecting” it. For example, Field_4 already has four protecting drones (Drone_2, Drone_3, Drone_5, Drone_8) although its quota is only 2. Field_9 is already “covered” by six protecting drones. In contrast Field_12 – even though its threat is very high (0.16) – has no drone on it, so it “requires” six drones. 

Step 2. Because there is a shortage of extra drones, we “choose” to commit extra (idle) drones only to the fields with high threat. In our solution we reserve our available idle drones for those fields with threat levels at (roughly) 0.07 or higher. (All non–high–threat fields then remain unprotected except for those already in the process of protecting or moving.) 

Step 3. For every field that “needs protection” we retain the drones already “protecting” it (and the ones already “moving” into it). 

Step 4. Then we “assign” extra idle drones – choosing those that are “closest” as judged roughly by our ordering – to cover the extra protection needed. In our solution we imagine that the idle drones in our “list” (when sorted by increasing drone number) are “geographically near” the respective fields. For example, we assign the very first six idle drones (Drone_1, Drone_4, Drone_6, Drone_7, Drone_9 and Drone_10) to Field_12; then the next nine idle drones to Field_14; then the next six to Field_22; next six to Field_13; next six to Field_29; next four to Field_25; next three to Field_27; next six to Field_33; next two to Field_68; next two to Field_1; next six to Field_21; then one to Field_66; one to Field_36; two to Field_40; two to Field_56; two to Field_59; then two more are dedicated (from our idle “pool”) to Field_2 and finally six to Field_17. (This “assignment” totals 73 of our idle drones. Any remaining idle drones we leave in the idle group.) 

Step 5. Finally, we give the final group assignment for every drone. (Drones already “protecting” a field or “moving” toward it keep their originally indicated target‐field group; only idle drones get “re–assigned” if we decide to use an extra drone to fill in a high–threat field’s remaining quota.)

Below is our final answer. (Each line is in the requested format “<name>: <group>”. Drones that were already in a protection state keep their field and moving drones keep theirs. The extra idle drones we “assign” following our list appear with a protecting group; any remaining idle drone is simply left “idle”.)

