Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and then the final assignment. 

–––– Step 1. For each field we looked at the “protecting” drones already assigned and compared that with the field’s full‐protection requirement. For example, Field_1 (threat 0.09) already has two protecting drones (Drone_54 and Drone_55) so no extra ones are needed; Field_2 (threat 0.10) has six protecting drones so it is “covered” already; Field_3 and Field_4 have protecting drones but since their threat level is 0 we have decided that later those drones will be released (set to idle). In addition, fields with a nonzero threat but still unprotected – for example Field_5 (threat 0.05 needing 2 drones) and Field_7 (threat 0.04 needing 1 drone) – were marked as “in need” so that extra drones must be assigned.

–––– Step 2. Next we ranked the fields by their threat level and – given that we do not have enough drones to cover every field – we choose to protect only those with a moderate–to–high threat. In our review the fields we decide to cover are those that already were partly or fully protected (Field_1, Field_2, Field_6 and Field_9) plus we add protection for Field_5 and Field_7 (low but already “in‐play”) and for the higher–priority fields whose threat level is above about 0.10. In our plan we decide to protect (by re–assigning drones from groups whose fields aren’t threatened) also Field_12 (threat 0.11), Field_13 (0.12), Field_14 (0.19), Field_21 (0.12), Field_22 (0.14), Field_33 (0.13), Field_34 (0.16) and Field_42 (0.10). Finally Field_68 (0.12) is already being “sent” two drones so we leave that assignment intact.

–––– Step 3. For each field that already had drones protecting it and that we want to protect, we “lock‐in” those assignments. Thus Field_1 keeps Drone_54 and Drone_55; Field_2 keeps Drone_60, Drone_61, Drone_77, Drone_80, Drone_84 and Drone_85; Field_6 keeps five “protecting” drones but since we decided that three are enough we choose the three closest (we keep Drone_71, Drone_74 and Drone_87 as protecting, and reassign the others to idle); Field_9 already has more than enough protecting drones so we choose to keep six of those (we keep Drone_28, Drone_29, Drone_31, Drone_39, Drone_41 and Drone_42 as protecting).

–––– Step 4. For each field that needs protection but currently has no “protecting” drones, we pick the extra drones from those that are already en‐route (or will soon be idle because they were assigned to fields with zero threat). For example, for Field_5 (needing 2) we choose two of the drones that were moving originally toward Field_5 (we choose Drone_43 and Drone_45). For Field_7 (needing 1) we choose the closest among those that were originally heading to Field_7 (Drone_23). Then, for several of the high–threat fields (Field_12, Field_13, Field_14, Field_21, Field_22, Field_33, Field_34 and Field_42) we select available drones from groups originally en–route to fields we later released. (In our planning we selected, for example, a block of drones originally moving toward Field_2 to cover Field_14 and Field_21; and drones originally headed for Field_3 or Field_4 to cover Field_12 and Field_13; and a remaining few for Field_33, Field_34 and Field_42.) 
Any drone that was originally protecting a field that in our plan is not among the ones in need of protection (or that was en–route to an “over–protected” field) is re–assigned to the “idle” group.

–––– Step 5. In our final answer each drone gets exactly one group – either one of the “protecting Field_X” groups if it has been selected to cover one of the chosen fields, or “idle” if it is not needed. (Note that we made arbitrary but plausible choices from among drones that were “on the way” so that the most threatened fields get exactly the number of drones needed.)

Below is our final assignment. Every drone appears on one line with its chosen group. 

