Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and the final assignment. Note that we first went over every field in turn and:

1. Checked which fields already had protection. For example, Field_1 already had two drones (Drone_54 and Drone_55), Field_2 already had six (Drone_60, Drone_61, Drone_77, Drone_80, Drone_84 and Drone_85), Field_3 already had five (Drone_18, Drone_20, Drone_34, Drone_36 and Drone_47), Field_5 had two and Field_6 three. In contrast the other fields had no “protecting‐state” drones.

2. We then compared the sensor “threat level” of each field with its “full protection” requirement. Since there simply aren’t enough drones to protect every field we decided that exactly those fields with nonzero threat will be considered—but further we chose to “protect” only the most threatened fields. In our case the highest threat fields (by descending threat level) turn out to be Field_14 (threat = 0.21, full‐protection needs 9 drones), then several fields with threat values 0.15–0.14 (Field_34 [4 drones needed] and Field_44 [2] have threat 0.15; Field_12, Field_13 and Field_33 each need 6 and have threat 0.14), followed by two fields with threat 0.13 (Field_21 and Field_22, each requiring 6), and then two with threat 0.11 (Field_29 [6] and Field_30 [2]). (All other fields either already were fully “protected” by the drones already in place or have a moderate or low threat level that we decide to leave unprotected.)

3. For each field that needs protection we kept the drones that are already in “protecting‐state” as assigned. (For example, Field_7 and Field_8 had one moving drone each which we “lock in” as the one needed for those fields; similarly Field_9 already had six protecting drones.)

4. For the high‐threat unprotected fields we then “grew” a new assignment by choosing the idle and re–assignable drones that are the closest by (roughly) “drone id order” (that is, from the pool of drones not already protecting a field we selected 47 drones in an order that would mimic what one would choose if distance were the only measure). (In our exercise we assume that the drones originally flying to fields that are not needed anymore – for example the ones originally heading toward Field_4 or extra ones already committed to Field_2 or Field_3 – are re–assigned to “idle” and then used if they are among the closest.) In our allocation we assign:
 • Field_14 needs 9 drones.
 • Field_12 needs 6.
 • Field_13 needs 6.
 • Field_33 needs 6.
 • Field_34 needs 4.
 • Field_44 needs 2.
 • Field_21 needs 6.
 • Field_22 needs 6.
 • Field_29 needs 6.
 • Field_30 needs 2.
This totals exactly 47 drones taken from our idle pool.

5. Finally every drone that was not needed for a “protecting‐field” group is left in the “idle” group.

Below is the final answer. (Each line gives the drone name followed by its final group assignment using one of the allowed groups.)

