Below is how I “worked‐through” the process. (Note that in a real‐life decision there is a lot of “fuzziness” about exactly which moving drone is “closest” so here I made an executive‐decision that because our entire fleet is “short” relative to all fields that we protect only the very highest–threat fields receive extra “protecting” orders (and all other drones are reassigned to “idle”). In our “step‐by‐step” reasoning I first noted that many fields are already “covered” by often more than the requested number; then I determined (by “ranking” the fields by their current threat level) that if we have only 72 drones available to “upgrade protection” on fields that are not yet protected at all we can only cover about 71 protection “slots” in total. In my ranking the highest–threat fields were Field_14 (threat 0.21, which requires 9 drones), Field_29 (0.19, 6 drones), Field_46 (0.18, …), Field_34, Field_33, Field_11, Field_12, Field_10, Field_13, Field_27, Field_52, Field_28, Field_68, Field_56, Field_64, Field_66, Field_21, Field_25 – in descending order. Because only 72 “new” protecting assignments are available, I chose to “fill” the protection “slots” only for the two very top‐prority sets – namely, I selected nine drones (the “closest” available from our moving/idle pool, by lowest drone id order) and assigned them to Field_14 and then six drones to Field_29. All other drones (even though some originally were “moving_to_field” for a field that would eventually have needed extra protection) are re–directed into idle. (Remember: partial protection is not effective – so if we cannot fill the entire “full” protection number for a given field we prefer to leave that field unassigned rather than add a “few” extra.)

So, in my final “group assignment” every drone that already was protecting a field that meets or exceeds its needed protection simply stays on that field (for example, the ten drones already protecting Field_4 remain in group “protecting Field_4”). From among all moving/idle drones I take the lowest–numbered eight available from the Field_2/Field_3/Field_4 moving sets – that is drones 11,12,13,14,46,53,69,86,88 (nine drones) – and assign them to Field_14. Then from the remaining available drones I assign six to Field_29 (drones 89,93,94,95,97,98). All other drones (including those that had been “moving_to_field” for fields that now are not receiving extra protection and every idle drone) are then set to idle.

