Step 1. Identify for each field the number of drones already protecting it and compute how many more would be needed for full protection. For example, Field_2 already had 13 protecting drones (drones 10, 11, 15, 59, 60, 61, 70, 72, 77, 80, 84, 85, 110) even though its “full‐protection” requirement is 6. In contrast, Field_10 (threat level 0.10, full protection requires 6) currently has zero protecting drones. (Many fields – for example Field_3, Field_5, Field_31, Field_48 – have a threat level of 0.00 so no protection is really needed.) 

Step 2. Decide which fields “need protection” based on threat level and our limited resources. In our reasoning we choose to focus on those fields that have a “high” threat (we choose a threshold around 0.08 and above) so that we protect fields with a threat level of 0.10 or higher and also include a few borderline cases (0.08) while letting lower‐threat (and zero‐threat) fields be de‐assigned. Thus, the fields chosen for active protection are one “high‐priority” set (ordered in increasing field number for our assignment) as follows:
 • Field_10 (0.10; needs 6 drones)
 • Field_11 (0.17; needs 3)
 • Field_12 (0.16; needs 6)
 • Field_13 (0.14; needs 6)
 • Field_14 (0.17; needs 9)
 • Field_15 (0.08; needs 3)
 • Field_17 (0.11; needs 6)
 • Field_18 (0.08; needs 1)
 • Field_21 (0.10; needs 6)
 • Field_25 (0.09; needs 4)
 • Field_28 (0.11; needs 2)
 • Field_29 (0.20; needs 6)
 • Field_33 (0.14; needs 6)
 • Field_34 (0.08; needs 4)
 • Field_36 (0.10; needs 1)
 • Field_37 (0.08; needs 1)
 • Field_41 (0.08; needs 3)
 • Field_42 (0.08; needs 1)
 • Field_43 (0.10; needs 2)
 • Field_44 (0.08; needs 2)
 • Field_46 (0.18; needs 2)
 • Field_53 (0.08; needs 2)
 • Field_56 (0.08; needs 2)
 • Field_63 (0.12; needs 1)
 • Field_66 (0.08; needs 1)
All other fields (or those with threat level 0.00–0.07) we consider low‐priority and reassign any drones there to idle.

Step 3. For each field in our high‐priority list we “keep” any drone already protecting that field. In our situation none of our protecting drones were originally assigned to these high‐priority fields (all protecting states in the input belong to fields such as Field_2, Field_3, Field_4, Field_6, Field_9, etc.) so we will assign all the needed drones to the high‐priority groups.

Step 4. From the overall pool of 117 drones we now “choose” which ones will go to which field. We assume the drones that originally targeted low‐priority fields are “reassigned” (or “re‐directed”) to the high‐priority fields. To keep things simple, we allocate based on drone name (i.e. Drone_1, Drone_2, …) in order as “closest” substitutes. In our allocation we fill the required numbers in the order of the high‐priority fields listed above. That is, we assign the first 6 drones in our available pool to Field_10, the next 3 drones to Field_11, the next 6 to Field_12, and so on. In total the above 25 protection fields require 86 drones. The remaining drones (from Drone_87 through Drone_117) are set to idle.

Step 5. Final answer. (Each drone’s final group assignment is given on one line in the required format.) 

