──────────────────────────── Step 3. For each “chosen” field, we keep already–protecting drones intact.
In our situation several fields already had protecting drones (for example Field_2, Field_3, Field_4). But since these fields are not chosen (their threat level is very low) we now have all drones from them available to reassign. Meanwhile the fields that are chosen already have some “protecting” drones (if any) and we “keep” them for full protection. (For Field_7, although originally seven drones were headed there as moving or protecting, we decide to keep only one – the one that is “closest” – while the extra ones are re‐assigned idle.) We do a similar “pruning” for Field_8 and so on.

──────────────────────────── Step 4. Now add (using the closest available idle drone “resources”) as many drones as needed for chosen fields.
For every chosen field we assign exactly the required number of drones – keeping one drone if a field requires only one even when many were already en‐route.
